# Chroma library, modern Earth: THE SPHERES OF SOCIETY, title moments of the spheres' first jobs. Library thread,
# 2026-10-09, "v22" update stage 4, item 15 (chroma-ideas/spheres-implementation.md section 4 "Library", "First jobs").
# An everyday pair per new career title of first_jobs_titles.py that only its holder meets (holds:), in the style of
# earth-titles-careers.lib: the first moment grows from the title's turning point, the second is a later stretch of
# the same job. The three first jobs that an existing title already is (bar hand, orderly, apprentice) get no moments
# here: their titles are tagged instead (TAG_EXISTING).
# Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: SPEC.md. Every moment carries sphere: <sphere>, every option the face it acts in
# (a cross option X>Y the means colour's face). ladder: is the rung the job sits on in that face's ladder
# (character.jobs, read rung by rung); haunt: where the job is done at a haunt among its regulars.
# Evenness: every one-colour option has chance 75 and every cross option 65, so each colour's mean is the same.
# Pairs (complementary calls, cross cycles s1 to s4 three times each):
#   court clerk     WB (s1) / URG (s2)      session player  UR (s3) / WBG (s4)     alms visitor  WG (s1) / UBR (s2)
#   private tutor   BR (s3) / WUG (s4)      market porter   WB (s1) / URG (s2)     door staff    BR (s3) / WUG (s4)
# holds: names the new titles, which are not yet in earth_perks_titles.py (they wait for FIRST_JOBS to be merged).

# ================================================================ court clerk (rule.W)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls WB (cycle s1); partner: the same name on the list again

== the missing page on list day
tier: everyday
sphere: rule
ladder: known
at: court
rate: 0.5
share: .005
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-70
calls: WB
holds: court clerk
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: moment
roles: colleague, boss
earth: on a busy list day a claim from a man with no lawyer is missing a page, and you know where the page went
times: Most court clerks, about once a year; misfiled papers are a routine trouble of a busy court list, and court staff are about 1 worker in 400 (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: It is list day, and a claim from a man with no lawyer is missing its second page. The bench will strike it out at eleven. {N} saw that page clipped to the wrong file yesterday.
scene W: {N} took the post to see every claim filed right, whoever brings it. A man without a lawyer has no one else to notice.
scene U: {N} works it back: the page went into the next file in the tray, and that file went up to the judge's room at nine.
scene B: The clerk who misfiled it is {colleague}, who sits on the panel that picks next year's senior clerk.
scene R: Through the glass {N} can see the man on the bench outside, turning his cap in his hands. Eleven is twenty minutes away.
scene G: The old clerks always said the court belongs to the town, not to the lawyers. {N} has seen this man's family in the waiting room before.
- W | ask the bench to stand the claim down until the page is found | v: universalism, conformity | lever: voice | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | trace the page through the morning's file trays before eleven | v: achievement | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | fetch the page from upstairs quietly, and say nothing of who lost it | v: security, power | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | go out to the waiting bench and tell the man straight what has happened | v: benevolence, self-direction | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | find the man's cousin in the waiting room and have the family bring a copy | v: tradition, benevolence | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- W>U | check every claim on today's list for missing pages before the bench sits | v: conformity, achievement | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U>B | write down where the page turned up, so no one can pin the loss on you | act: write down where the page turned up, so no one can pin the loss on them | v: security | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B>R | keep your head down, let the claim fall, and get the list done by lunch | act: keep their head down, let the claim fall, and get the list done by lunch | v: hedonism, security | closed: approval: letting a claim fail that a clerk could save; backfire: the man appeals and the file shows the page | mark: refused someone in need | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R>G | run upstairs for the page and hand it to the man in front of his family | v: benevolence, stimulation | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G>W | follow the old clerks' custom: a written note to the bench vouching for the claim | v: tradition, universalism | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
works: The page is back in the file by ten to eleven, and the claim is heard that morning.
works: The bench thanks {N} from the chair, and the man nods at {N} on his way out.
fails: The claim is struck out before the page turns up, and the man has to start again.
fails: {colleague} hears who raised it, and the office is cold to {N} for a month.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls URG (cycle s2); partner: the missing page on list day

== the same name on the list again
tier: everyday
sphere: rule
ladder: known
at: court
rate: 0.5
share: .004
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-70
calls: URG
holds: court clerk
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss, neighbour
earth: a young person from your own street is on the court list for the third time this year, again with no lawyer
times: Most court clerks in a town court, once or twice in their years there; the same young people come back on small charges, and clerks often know the families (estimate)
gap: 1-3
scene: For the third time this year {N} types the same name onto the list: a nineteen-year-old from {Ns} own street, on a small charge, again with no lawyer.
scene W: The clerk's rule is to treat every name on the list the same, and to help none of them more than the others.
scene U: Three small charges in a year, all on a Friday night, all near the same bar. {N} wonders what keeps bringing them back.
scene B: If anyone in the office learns that {N} knows the family, {N} may be moved off the court, or worse.
scene R: {N} remembers this kid kicking a ball against the garages. It makes {N} angry to see them walk in alone again.
scene G: Their grandmother still sits on the bench outside the corner shop. Everyone on the street will hear how it goes.
- W | treat them exactly as every other name on the list, no more and no less | v: conformity, universalism | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | look back through the files to see what keeps bringing them here | v: universalism | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | ask to be moved off that court for the day, to keep your name clear | act: ask to be moved off that court for the day, to keep their name clear | v: security | lever: exit | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | catch them in the corridor and tell them to get a lawyer this time | v: benevolence, self-direction | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | tell their grandmother on the street that the free advice desk opens at nine | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- W>B | ask the court manager, by the book, whether you should declare that you know them | act: ask the court manager, by the book, whether they should declare that they know the family | v: conformity, security | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U>R | point them to the duty lawyer's leaflet, the one most people never read | v: universalism, self-direction | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B>G | have a word about them with a friend in the probation office | v: benevolence, power | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R>W | speak up at the staff meeting about how many young people come back alone | v: universalism | lever: voice | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G>U | ask the older clerks which local schemes kept young people out of court before | v: tradition, universalism | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
works: The case is heard fairly, and the nineteen-year-old leaves with a lawyer's card and a second chance.
works: Months later {N} notices the name has not come back on the list, and says nothing to anyone.
fails: The name is back on the list in the spring, and this time the charge is not small.
fails: {boss} hears that {N} knows the family, and {N} spends a week explaining it in the office.

# ================================================================ session player (arts.U)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls UR (cycle s3); partner: the paid session on the night of the show

== a better line on the second take
tier: everyday
sphere: arts
haunt: arts.song
ladder: regular
at: employer
rate: 0.5
share: .003
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-80
calls: UR
holds: session player
tone: mixed
life: work, play
horizon: moment
roles: boss, colleague, mentor
earth: you are the hired player on someone else's record, the producer wants the part exactly as written, and you hear a better one
times: Most session players, a few times a year; how much of a written part a hired player may change is the commonest question of studio work (estimate)
gap: 0-0.5
scene: Three hours are booked, and {N} is the hired player on someone else's record. The producer wants the part exactly as written. On the second take {N} hears a better line in it.
scene W: The band hired {N} to serve the song, not to show off. The sheet is on the stand.
scene U: The written part sits a fraction behind the beat. {N} can hear exactly where it fights the singer's voice.
scene B: A producer who likes {N} today rings again next month. A producer who is crossed rings someone else.
scene R: The band are flat and tired, and the line in {Ns} head would wake the whole room up.
scene G: {mentor} played sessions for thirty years and always said the song comes first, and the old records know what a song needs.
- W | play the part exactly as written, take after take, until the producer is happy | v: conformity, achievement | sphere: arts.W | chance: 75
- U | ask for one take your way, then let the playback decide | act: ask for one take their way, then let the playback decide | v: achievement, self-direction | sphere: arts.U | chance: 75
- B | play it as asked, and leave your number with the singer on the way out | act: play it as asked, and leave their number with the singer on the way out | v: achievement, power | sphere: arts.B | chance: 75
- R | play the new line on the next take without asking | v: self-direction, stimulation | sphere: arts.R | chance: 75
- G | play it the way the old records played songs like this one | v: tradition | sphere: arts.G | chance: 75
- W>R | keep to the written part, but give it everything on the last take | v: stimulation, conformity | sphere: arts.W | chance: 65
- U>G | learn the singer's phrasing first, and build the part around their breath | v: benevolence, achievement | sphere: arts.U | chance: 65
- B>W | agree the fee for an extra hour before offering any new ideas | v: security, power | sphere: arts.B | chance: 65
- R>U | play the line ten ways at the break until you find the one that fits | act: play the line ten ways at the break until they find the one that fits | v: stimulation, achievement | sphere: arts.R | chance: 65
- G>B | mention who taught you, and let the old name open the door | act: mention who taught them, and let the old name open the door | v: tradition, power | sphere: arts.G | chance: 65
works: The take is the one they keep, and the producer writes {Ns} name on the studio's short list.
works: The singer hears the playback, grins, and asks {N} back for the next record.
fails: The producer stops the take halfway and asks, coldly, for the part as written.
fails: The session runs over, the band blame the hired player, and the phone stays quiet for a month.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls WBG (cycle s4); partner: a better line on the second take

== the paid session on the night of the show
tier: everyday
sphere: arts
haunt: arts.song
ladder: regular
at: employer
rate: 0.5
share: .002
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-80
calls: WBG
holds: session player
tone: trouble
life: work, friends
horizon: week
roles: friend, boss
earth: two nights of paid studio work land on the same night your old band plays its first real show
times: Most session players, once or twice early on; paid work and one's own band pull on the same nights (estimate)
gap: 1-3
scene: A studio rings with two nights of paid work, starting Friday. Friday is also the first real show for the band {N} started at school with {friend}.
scene W: {N} promised {friend} months ago, and the posters with {Ns} name on them are already up.
scene U: {N} counts it out: the session pays more than the band has earned in a year, and the studio's players are the best in town.
scene B: Session work goes to whoever says yes first. Say no once, and the phone may not ring again.
scene R: The show is what {N} has dreamed of since fourteen. The session is just a job.
scene G: The band are {Ns} oldest friends, and their families will all be in the crowd.
- W | keep your promise and play the show | act: keep their promise and play the show | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | sphere: arts.W | chance: 75
- U | ask the studio whether the session could start on Saturday instead | v: achievement | sphere: arts.U | chance: 75
- B | take the session and find the band a stand-in player | v: achievement, security | mark: broke your word | sphere: arts.B | chance: 75
- R | play both: the show at nine, then the night session straight after | v: stimulation, hedonism | sphere: arts.R | chance: 75
- G | ask the band what they want you to do, and do that | act: ask the band what they want them to do, and do that | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: arts.G | chance: 75
- W>G | play the show, and bring the band to meet the studio players another night | v: benevolence | sphere: arts.W | chance: 65
- U>W | offer the studio a fellow player you trust, so no one is let down | act: offer the studio a fellow player they trust, so no one is let down | v: benevolence, universalism | sphere: arts.U | chance: 65
- B>U | take the session, and learn everything the best players in town do | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | sphere: arts.B | chance: 65
- R>B | tell the studio the show matters, and ask double for a Saturday start | v: power, stimulation | sphere: arts.R | chance: 65
- G>R | turn down the money and play with your oldest friends as if for the last time | act: turn down the money and play with their oldest friends as if for the last time | v: tradition, hedonism | sphere: arts.G | chance: 65
works: The night works out both ways, and the studio rings again the week after.
works: The band play the best show of their lives, and {friend} hugs {N} on the pavement after.
fails: The studio finds another player and does not ring again that year.
fails: {friend} hears how close {N} came to not turning up, and the band is never quite the same.

# ================================================================ alms visitor (faith.W)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls WG (cycle s1); partner: the family outside the congregation

== the door that stays shut on the visiting list
tier: everyday
sphere: faith
haunt: faith.congregation
ladder: newcomer
at: faith body
rate: 0.5
share: .003
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-85
calls: WG
holds: alms visitor
tone: trouble
life: work, faith, community
horizon: week
roles: elder, neighbour
earth: an old man on your visiting list has stopped opening his door, and the alms fund drops anyone not seen this month
times: Most alms visitors, once or twice a year; on any visiting list someone withdraws for a while (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: The old man at number twelve has not opened his door for three weeks. The alms fund pays only for those seen this month, and the form is due on Friday.
scene W: The fund's rule is fair because it is the same for all. It is also about to leave a man without his shopping.
scene U: The curtains move when {N} knocks, and the milk is gone from the step each morning. Something has changed, and {N} wants to know what.
scene B: The committee chooses a paid coordinator next month, and they notice whose forms come in clean.
scene R: {N} wants to bang on the door until he answers.
scene G: He sat two rows ahead of {Ns} grandparents in the hall for forty years. Someone on the street will know him.
- W | write on the form exactly what you saw, and ask the committee for a month's grace | act: write on the form exactly what they saw, and ask the committee for a month's grace | v: conformity, benevolence | lever: voice | sphere: faith.W | chance: 75
- U | ask the neighbours when they last saw him, and piece together what changed | v: universalism, benevolence | sphere: faith.U | chance: 75
- B | sign the form as seen, and sort out the truth next month | v: security | closed: approval: signing for a visit that did not happen; backfire: the committee checks the list | mark: hid a wrong | sphere: faith.B | chance: 75
- R | push a note under the door and wait on the step until he answers | v: benevolence, self-direction | sphere: faith.R | chance: 75
- G | ask the oldest members at the hall whom he still talks to | v: tradition, benevolence | sphere: faith.G | chance: 75
- W>U | ring the council's welfare line and ask for a proper check on him | v: universalism, security | sphere: faith.W | chance: 65
- U>B | look up what other help he is owed, so the fund's rule matters less | v: security, universalism | sphere: faith.U | chance: 65
- B>R | leave the bag on the step and get through the rest of the list early | v: hedonism, security | sphere: faith.B | chance: 65
- R>G | knock every evening this week with a hot meal from home | v: benevolence | sphere: faith.R | chance: 65
- G>W | bring his old friend from the hall along on the next visit | v: tradition, benevolence | sphere: faith.G | chance: 65
works: On the fourth day the door opens a crack, and the old man asks {N} in for tea.
works: The committee grants the month's grace, and the shopping keeps coming while he finds his feet.
fails: The fund drops him from the list, and {N} carries his shopping out of {Ns} own wage for a month.
fails: The door stays shut, and {N} learns from a neighbour that his son has taken him away to another town.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls UBR (cycle s2); partner: the door that stays shut on the visiting list

== the family outside the congregation
tier: everyday
sphere: faith
haunt: faith.congregation
ladder: newcomer
at: faith body
rate: 0.5
share: .003
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-85
calls: UBR
holds: alms visitor
tone: mixed
life: work, faith, community
horizon: week
roles: elder, neighbour
earth: a family outside the congregation asks you for help, and the alms fund's rules put members first
times: Most alms visitors, once or twice a year; a fund kept for members meets need on the street outside it (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: A young mother from the flats stops {N} on the stairs. She does not belong to the congregation, her children need shoes, and the fund's rules say members come first.
scene W: The rule was written so the fund would last. {N} also remembers the old saying that the alms door shuts on no one.
scene U: {N} knows the fund had money left over last winter. Nobody has asked whether the rule still fits the street.
scene B: Bending the rule once makes {N} the visitor everyone in the flats asks next.
scene R: The children stand behind her in their socks on the cold stairs. {N} does not want to quote a rule at them.
scene G: Her grandmother came to the hall when {N} was small. The family drifted away, but the street remembers them.
- W | explain the rule kindly, and put her name first on the list for spring | v: conformity, benevolence | sphere: faith.W | chance: 75
- U | take her case to the committee with last winter's figures in hand | v: universalism, achievement | lever: voice | sphere: faith.U | chance: 75
- B | give her the address of the council's help desk, and keep the fund's rule | v: security, conformity | sphere: faith.B | chance: 75
- R | buy the shoes out of your own wage that afternoon | act: buy the shoes out of their own wage that afternoon | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | sphere: faith.R | chance: 75
- G | ask the hall's old members to pass round a hat for the family | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: fund | sphere: faith.G | chance: 75
- W>B | sign her up as a member's guest, which the rules allow, and note it properly | v: conformity, benevolence | sphere: faith.W | chance: 65
- U>R | find a children's shoe fund online and fill in the form with her | v: universalism, self-direction | tech: internet | sphere: faith.U | chance: 65
- B>G | call in a favour from the shoe shop owner, who owes the hall a kindness | v: power, benevolence | sphere: faith.B | chance: 65
- R>W | stand up at the next meeting and ask for the members-first rule to go | v: universalism | lever: voice | sphere: faith.R | chance: 65
- G>U | ask her grandmother's old friends what the family needs most, and plan with them | v: tradition, universalism | sphere: faith.G | chance: 65
works: The children have shoes by the weekend, and their mother comes to the hall's supper the next month.
works: The committee changes the rule at its next meeting, and {Ns} name is in the minutes.
fails: The committee says no, and the mother stops {N} on the stairs no more.
fails: Word gets round the flats, and by Friday {N} has a list of twenty families and nothing to give them.

# ================================================================ private tutor (learn.B)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls BR (cycle s3); partner: the pupil who cannot pay any more

== a parent asks you to write the coursework
tier: everyday
sphere: learn
ladder: newcomer
rate: 0.5
share: .03
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-80
calls: BR
holds: private tutor
tone: trouble
life: work, money
horizon: week
roles: mentor, a parent of a pupil
earth: a parent paying double asks you to write the coursework their child is meant to write, and your rent is due
times: About half of private tutors, once in their years of tutoring; paid help that slides into doing the work is a known trouble of private tuition (estimate)
gap: 1-3
scene: At the door, the mother of {Ns} best-paying pupil asks whether {N} could just write the coursework this once. She will pay double, and {Ns} rent is due on Friday.
scene W: The coursework is meant to show what the pupil can do. Every other pupil in the class writes their own.
scene U: {N} knows the pupil could do it in three good sessions, if someone broke the task into pieces.
scene B: Double the fee covers the rent, and a happy parent sends their friends' children.
scene R: {N} has had enough of evenings spent on a teenager who will not look up from the phone.
scene G: {mentor} once tutored {N} for nothing, and would never have taken money to do a child's work.
- W | say no, and offer the pupil an extra session at the usual fee | v: conformity, benevolence | sphere: learn.W | chance: 75
- U | split the task into steps, and set the pupil one step a night | v: achievement | sphere: learn.U | chance: 75
- B | take the double fee and write it | v: achievement, power | closed: approval: writing a pupil's coursework for pay; backfire: the school spots it | mark: hid a wrong | sphere: learn.B | chance: 75
- R | tell the mother straight that you are a tutor, not a ghost writer | act: tell the mother straight that they are a tutor, not a ghost writer | v: self-direction | sphere: learn.R | chance: 75
- G | ring your old tutor and ask how they handled parents like this | act: ring their old tutor and ask how they handled parents like this | v: tradition | sphere: learn.G | chance: 75
- W>R | say no, drop the family, and take your Thursday evenings back | act: say no, drop the family, and take their Thursday evenings back | v: self-direction, conformity | sphere: learn.W | chance: 65
- U>G | show the pupil how their own family's story could be the coursework | v: tradition, achievement | sphere: learn.U | chance: 65
- B>W | write a model answer on another topic, which the rules allow, and charge for it | v: achievement, conformity | sphere: learn.B | chance: 65
- R>U | make the coursework a race: who writes the best opening line by nine | v: stimulation, achievement | sphere: learn.R | chance: 65
- G>B | hand the family to a friend who tutors, who will owe you one | act: hand the family to a friend who tutors, who will owe them one | v: power, benevolence | sphere: learn.G | chance: 65
works: The pupil hands in work of their own, and it is the best mark they have had all year.
works: The mother takes the answer well, and a fortnight later two of her friends ring for lessons.
fails: The family find another tutor by the weekend, and the rent is short that month.
fails: The pupil's teacher asks questions about the work, and {Ns} name comes up.

